            0.086
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.108,
            0.889,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "So far, we have only considered points on the two coordinate axes. What can you say about the coordinates of points that are not on either axes? The plane in which the axes are situated is called the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Cartesian plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ", the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "coordinate plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " or the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "xy-plane",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". The axes divide the plane into four parts, called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "quadrants",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". They are numbered as shown in Fig. 1.4."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.223,
            0.701,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Using the conventions that we have stated earlier:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.12,
            0.247,
            0.833,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Points in Quadrant I have both x- and y-coordinates positive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.272,
            0.888,
            0.313
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Points in Quadrant II have negative x-coordinate and positive y-coordinate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.318,
            0.858,
            0.34
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) Points in Quadrant III have both x- and y-coordinates negative."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.342,
            0.888,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) Points in Quadrant IV have positive x-coordinate and negative y-coordinate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.392,
            0.89,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Can you now see the meaning of the coordinates of a point in 2-D space? In general, the coordinates of a point P in 2-D space are represented by (x, y). Here, x represents the perpendicular distance of P from the y-axis, measured along the x-axis, and y is the perpendicular distance of P from the x-axis, measured along the y-axis. x is the x-coordinate and y is the y-coordinate of the point (x, y)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
